digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N12" [label="(11,1,0) Previous proposals for utilizing atomic media to achieve negative refraction relied on quantum interference effects in multilevel systems."];
  "N13" [label="(11,2,0) Previous proposals involved inherently weak magnetic dipole transitions for independently scattering atoms."];
  "N14" [label="(12,1,0) Previous approaches necessitate exceptionally high refractive indices at high densities."];
  "N15" [label="(12,2,0) The exceptionally high refractive indices required surpass limits of current experimental capabilities."];
  "N16" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-case: Metamaterials with fabricated resonators have losses; 3D photonic crystals have constraints; previous atomic media proposals require experimentally unachievable conditions. Induction-common: All current approaches to negative refraction (metamaterials, photonic crystals, previous atomic proposals) face fundamental implementation barriers."];
  "N28" [label="(13,1,0) Negative refraction is demonstrated through microscopic atom-by-atom simulations."];
  "N29" [label="(13,2,0) Microscopic simulations exactly incorporate all recurrent scattering processes between stationary atoms in low light intensity limit."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Metamaterials are synthetic man-made media developed to overcome limitations of natural materials, with unconventional refraction phenomena as key driving force."];
  "N35" [label="(15,0,0) Simulations uncover distinct deflection of propagating light beams in opposite direction to what normally occurs."];
  "N4" [label="(4,1,0) Despite significant interest in negative refraction, intrinsic non-radiative damping in metamaterials results in significant losses at optical frequencies."];
  "N43" [label="(18,2,0) Refraction aligns with simple phenomenology of transverse Bloch band collective resonances."];
  "N44" [label="(19,1,0) Negative refraction can be realized by engineering resonance bands whose in-plane group velocities are antiparallel to excitation quasimomenta."];
  "N5" [label="(4,2,0) Fabrication imperfections of resonators in metamaterials result in significant losses at optical frequencies."];
  "N6" [label="(5,1,0) Negative refraction in 3D photonic crystals is constrained by refractive indices of constituent dielectrics."];
  "N7" [label="(5,2,0) Negative refraction in 3D photonic crystals is sensitive to imperfections."];
  "N8" [label="(5,3,0) Negative refraction in 3D photonic crystals operates within narrow range of incidence angles."];
  "N9" [label="(5,4,0) Negative refraction in 3D photonic crystals has limited subwavelength resolution due to wavelength-scale periodicity requirement."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate a resonator-free route to negative refraction of light by using cooperative, non-local optical responses in high-density subwavelength atomic arrays, with atom-by-atom simulations testing robust high-transmission behavior and transverse Bloch-band resonances whose in-plane group velocities are antiparallel to their quasimomenta.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When existing negative-refraction approaches face major limitations, and simulations plus mechanistic analysis support a robust cooperative atomic-array alternative, the coherent paper-level aim is to develop and evaluate that atomic-array route."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Microscopic atom-by-atom simulations that exactly include recurrent scattering and experimental conditions provide a suitable basis for evaluating light propagation and negative-refraction behavior in experimentally relevant atomic arrays."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Within the microscopic simulation framework, the distinct opposite-direction deflection of propagating light beams is evidence for simulated negative refraction in the atomic array."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The alignment of refraction with transverse Bloch-band collective resonances supports the interpretation that engineering resonance bands with in-plane group velocities antiparallel to excitation quasimomenta can realize negative refraction in the atomic array."];
